Let $\varphi=\angle DMO$. Since $I$ is equidistant from the lines $MO$ and $MD$, $MI$ bisects $\varphi$, so
\[
\tan\dfrac{\varphi}{2}=\dfrac{11}{r_0},\qquad \tan\varphi=\dfrac{24}{r_0}.
\]
By the double-angle formula, $\dfrac{22}{r_0}\div\left(1-\dfrac{121}{r_0^2}\right)=\dfrac{24}{r_0}$, so $22=24\left(1-\dfrac{121}{r_0^2}\right)$, which gives $\dfrac{121}{r_0^2}=\dfrac{1}{12}$ and $r_0^2=1452$, $r_0=22\sqrt3$. The inradius of an equilateral triangle with side $s$ is $\dfrac{s}{2\sqrt3}$, so
\[
AB=2\sqrt3\,r_0=2\sqrt3\cdot22\sqrt3=\boxed{132}.
\]
(Check with $r=\dfrac{3V}{\text{surface area}}$: the slant height is $DM=\sqrt{24^2+1452}=\sqrt{2028}=26\sqrt3$, the surface area is $\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot132^2+3\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot132\cdot26\sqrt3=4356\sqrt3+5148\sqrt3=9504\sqrt3$, and $V=\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot4356\sqrt3\cdot24=34848\sqrt3$, so $r=\dfrac{3\cdot34848}{9504}=11$.)
